# The three-Oblivion-Ring loop with Bot2 holding Disenchant and two untapped Plains, as in
# oblivion-ring-loop-a-player-can-break-with-an-instant-is-not-a-draw - but Bot2 never casts it. At every priority Bot2 could
# stop the loop, so it is no draw while Bot2 still may (CR 104.4b's last sentence: the loop goes round as long as Bot2
# passes, each round a chance to break it). But Disenchant is no part of the loop: no object of the loop calls for casting
# it, and no player can be made to take an action that would end a loop but those its objects call for (CR 732.5). The only
# thing Bot2 does in the loop is pass; the loop's own actions are all mandatory. Round after round Bot2 passes, the position
# coming back the same each time, until the loop has gone round far more often than anyone could mean to let it go round
# and then stop: it is a loop of mandatory actions (CR 732.4), and the game is a draw, Disenchant still in Bot2's hand.

